= Choosing our enemies

In our media and campaign materials, who should we consider as our main opponents?

[? if enemies != 0 : Previously, we had considered the [+ enemies +] as our prior enemies. Is a change of direction necessary? ?]

- @warlords: The despotic cliques of the Zhili, Anhui and Fengtian.
- @communists: The Communists, a danger to our republican ideals.
- @colonizers: The foreign powers who instilled unequal treaties among us!
- @easy_discard

@warlords
on-arrival: enemies = "warlords"; center_dissent -= 5; right_dissent -= 5; left_dissent -= 5; center_left_dissent -= 5; whampoa_dissent -= 7; rural_kmt += 1; cpc_relations += 5;

Attacking the warlords is popular among the party, since they are obstructing our way to government. This might also hamper our abilities to negotiate with other warlords.

@communists
on-arrival: enemies = "communists"; left_strength -= 5; left_dissent += 7; center_left_dissent += 5; right_dissent -= 5; whampoa_dissent -= 5; right_strength += 5; cpc_relations -= 5;

Attacking the communists make the Left and Center-left more angry since they support allying them instead. Although, it makes the Whampoa and Right happy.

@colonizers
on-arrival: enemies = "imperialists"; west_relations -= 5; east_relations -= 5; japan_relations -= 5; old_middle_kmt += 2; rural_kmt += 2;

Only focusing on the unequal treaties raises some public support, while it jeopardizes our foreign affairs.
